WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.200
The Simpsons has been around for so long,&nbsp;
the series can be used to mark the passage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:11.520
of time. In fact, plenty of the show's current&nbsp;
viewers were born after it debuted in 1989.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.160
If you're one of these fans, check out the most&nbsp;
popular episode from the year you were born.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.240
The most popular episode of The Simpsons&nbsp;
to air in 1989 was also the only one to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.440
air that year. Of course, we're talking about&nbsp;
"Simpsons Roasting on an Open Fire," the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.240
first full-length episode of what is now the&nbsp;
longest-running show in primetime history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.600
It's a classic Simpsons tale about Homer bumbling&nbsp;
through life in order to make his family happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:43.200
and it's packed with relatable moments, hilarious&nbsp;
disappointments, and cutting satirical humor.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.320
Early in the story, the Simpsons' Christmas&nbsp;
celebration is put in serious jeopardy when power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.760
plant owner Mr. Burns chooses fat executive&nbsp;
raises over holiday bonuses for employees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.000
Homer doesn't tell Marge the bad news,&nbsp;
because he thinks he can dip into a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.560
jar of money she has stashed in her hair.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, Marge ends up using this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.160
emergency fund on laser skin surgery for Bart&nbsp;
after he gets a tattoo when her back is turned.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.440
Forced to take on a second job for the holidays,&nbsp;
Homer becomes a lackluster mall Santa. He only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.880
ends up earning $13 and decides to bet it&nbsp;
all at the dog track, where he promptly loses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.640
everything. But, fear not, this first Simpsons&nbsp;
episode has a happy ending. After the race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.840
the losing dog that Homer bet on jumps into&nbsp;
his arms and Bart asks if they can keep him.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:36.400
"He's a loser. He's pathetic. He's...a Simpson."

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:43.120
In 1990, The Simpsons was just about the hottest&nbsp;
thing on television and Bart quickly became the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.360
breakout star. So it's not surprising that&nbsp;
he served as the centerpiece of the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:53.120
second-season premiere, "Bart Gets an 'F'," which&nbsp;
was also the most popular episode of the year.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.000
Appropriately enough, this installment forces&nbsp;
Bart to reckon with his immature decisions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.040
bad boy ways. After faking his way through a book&nbsp;
report on Treasure Island, which he didn't bother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.640
to read, and cheating off of Milhouse on a test,&nbsp;
the school psychiatrist tells Homer and Marge that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.000
it would be best for Bart to repeat the fourth&nbsp;
grade. Dead set against it, Bart actually tries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.160
to improve his marks, and gets class-brain Martin&nbsp;
to tutor him in exchange for popularity lessons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.200
After forcing himself to study, Bart defies&nbsp;
everyone's expectations and does well enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.600
on a history test to pass — though he&nbsp;
just barely scores a D minus. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.840
it was enough to earn his test a coveted place on&nbsp;
the fridge right next to one of Lisa's many A's.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.200
Viewers can pick up clues from early episodes of&nbsp;
The Simpsons that Homer and Marge are in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.880
late 30s, and met at Springfield High School.&nbsp;
The 1991 flashback episode "The Way We Was"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:52.400
provides a lot more detail by depicting the story&nbsp;
of Homer and Marge's first days as a couple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.960
The idea of a Simpsons episode set in the&nbsp;
70s, featuring a young Homer and Marge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:02.480
was irresistible and "The Way We Was" ended&nbsp;
up as the most-watched episode of 1991.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:08.720
Back in 1974, high school senior Homer lands in&nbsp;
detention yet again for smoking in the boys' room.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:14.000
Studious Marge winds up there for the first time,&nbsp;
because she burned a bra at a feminist rally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.560
When she walks into detention, it's love at&nbsp;
first sight for Homer, and he immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.360
wants to ask her to the prom. To get close&nbsp;
to Marge, he hires her as his French tutor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.760
despite not actually taking that class.&nbsp;
After an undeniable chemistry develops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:33.040
between them — and she accepts his prom invite —&nbsp;
he lets it slip that he lied about French class.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:37.920
Feeling betrayed, Marge goes to the prom&nbsp;
with handsy nerd Artie Ziff instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.080
standing up a heartbroken Homer. At the&nbsp;
end of an awful night for both of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:47.120
Marge picks up Homer off the side of the road,&nbsp;
and they share their first, fate-sealing kiss.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.800
While "Lisa's First Word" prominently&nbsp;
features Lisa as a baby, the real reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:58.240
this is the most-viewed episode of 1992 is that&nbsp;
it was a huge milestone in the life of Maggie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.360
In the run-up to this episode's airing, Fox&nbsp;
heavily hyped the fact that Hollywood legend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.680
and Academy Award-winner Elizabeth Taylor&nbsp;
would guest star as the voice of Maggie.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.240
Towards the beginning of the story, the Simpsons'&nbsp;
move into their home on Evergreen Terrace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:17.360
but that's just the first of many changes in store&nbsp;
for the family. Bart is also forced to contend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:22.960
with a creepy clown bed that Homer made because he&nbsp;
needs to give up his crib for the newly born Lisa.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:27.440
The new addition gets so much of his parents'&nbsp;
attention that Bart starts acting out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.400
threatens to run away. But he's stopped in&nbsp;
his tracks when Lisa says her first word:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.280
"Bart." At the end of the episode, the action&nbsp;
returns to the present, where Homer puts Maggie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:41.680
to bed, telling her he's in no rush for her to&nbsp;
speak because he doesn't want her to talk back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.680
After he leaves the room, Maggie delivers&nbsp;
the moment everyone was waiting for.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:47.600
"Daddy"

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:54.000
It's no secret that Homer is&nbsp;
a man of simple pleasures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.600
especially when it comes to alcohol. But in&nbsp;
"Duffless," the most-popular episode of 1993,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.480
Homer pays the price for his endless love of beer.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.280
As the story begins, Homer sneaks out of work&nbsp;
to take the Duff Brewery tour with Barney.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:12.000
Naturally, they both have a few samples,&nbsp;
rendering Barney physically unable to drive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.600
Homer, who didn't indulge as much&nbsp;
as Barney, gets behind the wheel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.360
and is immediately busted by Chief&nbsp;
Wiggum. Charged with drunk driving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.240
Homer is stripped of his license and forced&nbsp;
to attend Alcoholics Anonymous meetings.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.360
"My name is Homer and I'm just here&nbsp;
because the court made me come."

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.800
"Homer, with our help you'll&nbsp;
never touch a beer again."

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:39.680
Marge, worried about Homer's addictive behaviors,&nbsp;
thinks giving up beer for a month might be a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:45.440
idea for him. He reluctantly agrees, and though&nbsp;
it's extremely difficult, he eventually succeeds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:51.280
In the process, he saves $100 and loses so much&nbsp;
weight that he no longer sweats while eating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.320
At the end of the month, Homer even resists his&nbsp;
urge to down a few beers at the bar. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:01.520
he goes on a romantic bike ride with Marge. Let's&nbsp;
just hope he didn't end up taking her to Moe's.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:09.120
The Simpsons' annual Halloween specials are a TV&nbsp;
tradition that still endures to this day. In 1994,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:13.040
the phenomenon reached critical&nbsp;
mass as "Treehouse of Horror V,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:15.600
ranked as the most viewed episode of the year.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.560
The episode starts with a parody of the horror&nbsp;
classic The Shining, where Mr. Burns hires the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:25.360
Simpsons to be the winter caretakers of his remote&nbsp;
mountain lodge. But he makes sure to get rid of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:31.040
all the beer and TV sets, sending Homer into a&nbsp;
murderous rage. Then in "Time and Punishment,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:36.560
Homer tries to fix the family's toaster and&nbsp;
accidentally turns it into a time-travel device.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:40.640
After going back way too far and&nbsp;
squishing a prehistoric insect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:45.440
Homer zaps his way through various bizarre&nbsp;
timelines created by his actions, including a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:51.840
Ned Flanders-ruled dystopia. The episode finishes&nbsp;
out with "Nightmare Cafeteria." In this segment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:57.040
Principal Skinner helps Lunchlady Doris figure&nbsp;
out new ways to serve the kids on a budget.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:01.840
"Are you saying that you killed Jimbo, processed&nbsp;
his carcass, and served him for lunch?"

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:02.459
"Hah!"

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:11.760
While the owner of Springfield's Nuclear Power&nbsp;
Plant, Mr. Burns, is famously cruel, the 1995&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:18.000
episode "Who Shot Mr. Burns, Part 1" demonstrates&nbsp;
just how low the wicked tycoon is willing to sink.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:21.040
After oil is discovered under&nbsp;
Springfield Elementary School,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:26.480
bringing much-needed funds to the poorly-resourced&nbsp;
institution, Burns steals the lucrative crude with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:31.360
his "slant drilling" company. Not only does he&nbsp;
destroy large parts of the town in the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:36.960
including the Springfield Retirement Castle and&nbsp;
Moe's Tavern, he creates a monopoly on energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.960
forcing everyone in the region to&nbsp;
get their power from Burns' sources.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.560
By the end of the episode, Burns has&nbsp;
been shot by an unknown assailant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:50.000
and is barely clinging to life while sprawled out&nbsp;
on a sundial. Of course, this is only part one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:55.360
The Simpsons' most famous cliffhanger, which&nbsp;
left the world wondering "who shot Mr. Burns?"

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.280
"Well, I couldn't possibly&nbsp;
solve this mystery. Can you?"

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:03.520
"Yeah, I'll give it a shot."

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:08.720
Two years after earning some of its biggest&nbsp;
Halloween ratings ever, another Simpsons'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:15.280
fright-fest, "Treehouse of Horror VII," scared up&nbsp;
enough viewers to rank as the top episode of 1996.

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:19.680
Some of the interest in this episode might have&nbsp;
been influenced by that year's presidential race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:24.640
which was between Bill Clinton and Bob&nbsp;
Dole. The Simpsons, always a satirical show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:29.760
made fun of both candidates in this Halloween&nbsp;
special by having recurring alien invaders Kang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:35.040
and Kodos abduct the two men, take over their&nbsp;
bodies, and run for president in their place.

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:40.160
As the aliens point out, the United States has&nbsp;
a two-party system, so Americans have to vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:45.040
for one of them — and then wind up enslaved.&nbsp;
In the anthology episode's other segments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:50.800
Lisa's tooth-in-a-Petri-dish experiment leads to&nbsp;
a rapidly evolving society where she is worshiped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:53.680
and, in the final story,&nbsp;
the family confronts Hugo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:57.520
Bart's monstrous twin brother, who's been&nbsp;
kept chained up in the Simpson's attic.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:03.600
Fox was responsible for more than a few hits&nbsp;
that dominated pop culture in the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:10.080
but only one dared crossover with The Simpsons:&nbsp;
The X-Files. Bringing FBI agents Fox Mulder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:15.440
and Dana Scully to The Simpsons was, at its&nbsp;
core, a publicity stunt, and it worked like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:20.240
gangbusters: "The Springfield Files"&nbsp;
was the most popular episode of 1997.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.960
One night, a very drunk Homer walks home from&nbsp;
Moe's Tavern and stumbles into the woods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:28.560
where he comes face to face with&nbsp;
something completely unexplainable:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.120
A wispy, glowing, big-eyed creature&nbsp;
resembling popular depictions of aliens.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.520
News of the encounter reaches Mulder and Scully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:39.040
and they journey to Springfield to&nbsp;
investigate the mysterious sighting.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.560
Eventually, it's not the X-Files&nbsp;
duo that cracks the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:48.320
but Lisa. She forces Smithers to come forward&nbsp;
and explain that the presumed alien is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:52.880
the elderly and decrepit Mr. Burns. It&nbsp;
turns out that his weekly life-preserving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:57.600
medical treatments leave him so confused and&nbsp;
disoriented that he wanders around the woods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:01.680
The glow is simply a side effect from&nbsp;
working around nuclear power for so long.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:07.120
In "Bart Carny," the best-performing&nbsp;
episode of 1998, The Simpsons explore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:09.280
the sketchy world of traveling carnivals.

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:13.680
When the carnival comes to Springfield and the&nbsp;
family decides to check it out, Bart gets himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:18.800
into trouble almost immediately. He hijacks a&nbsp;
car that the carnival claims once belonged to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:24.720
Adolf Hitler and smashes it into a tree. To pay&nbsp;
for the damages, Bart and Homer become carnies.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:30.000
Under the tutelage of father-son carnie duo&nbsp;
Cooder and Spud, the Simpson guys learn all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.040
about fixing games and bribing police. Although,&nbsp;
Homer fails to properly pay off Chief Wiggum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:38.960
despite his many hints. This gets&nbsp;
Cooder and Spud's game shut down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:44.160
which is also where they live. Taking pity on&nbsp;
them, Homer invites them to stay with the family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:48.000
He soon learns that he shouldn't have&nbsp;
trusted the two carnies. Before long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:51.840
they temporarily assume ownership of the&nbsp;
house and won't let the Simpsons back in.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:59.840
"You can't do this to me.&nbsp;
What about the carnie code?

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:01.177
[mocking laughter]

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:09.120
In "Maximum Homerdrive," the most-watched episode&nbsp;
of 1999, Homer is intrigued by a promotional stunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:14.080
at a steakhouse: Whoever can eat a 16-pound&nbsp;
steak with all the trimmings gets it for free.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:18.160
He then accepts a challenge from a&nbsp;
long-haul truck driver named Red Barclay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:22.960
who bets Homer that he can finish the huge meal&nbsp;
before he does. Red is the first to polish off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:28.560
his steak, but in the process, he dies of what&nbsp;
Dr. Hibbert diagnoses as "beef poisoning."

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.280
Homer, taking all the wrong lessons from this&nbsp;
experience, decides there's only one thing to do:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:37.680
complete Red's last delivery&nbsp;
with Bart by his side. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:42.160
he does this without knowing how to drive a big&nbsp;
rig or understanding the culture of the road.

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:43.840
"He wants you to blow your horn."

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:44.340
"Oh."

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:49.680
"That little punk!"

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:54.720
It's a little surprising that it took until&nbsp;
The Simpsons's 12th season for trickster Bart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:59.200
to become a professional con artist. Fans&nbsp;
were apparently eager to experience this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:04.800
foregone conclusion firsthand, as "The Great&nbsp;
Money Caper" was the top episode of 2000.

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:09.200
At the beginning of the story, a sturgeon&nbsp;
falls from a space station and crashes into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:14.720
Simpsons' car. After that unexpected event, Bart&nbsp;
and Homer decide to busk as a magic act so they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:19.760
can earn enough money to fix the family car. The&nbsp;
scheme doesn't go according to plan and the duo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:25.360
only earns sixty cents. Homer quickly loses his&nbsp;
patience with Bart and drives away without him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:30.640
When passersby take pity on the boy, he starts&nbsp;
raking in the cash without having to do anything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:35.760
After Bart takes a taxi home and buys himself a&nbsp;
steak dinner, both Simpson guys realize that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:41.360
could just grift people to earn money, no busking&nbsp;
skills required. Naturally, it all goes bad and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:47.360
eventually, Homer and Bart get caught by an FBI&nbsp;
agent. But even that is a ruse. The agent is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:51.840
in fact, an actor hired by Marge&nbsp;
to teach Homer and Bart a lesson.

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:58.240
The 2001 episode "HOMR" attempts to explain why&nbsp;
the Simpsons' dad has such a unique personality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:01.360
to put it kindly. Fans were&nbsp;
so eager to learn how he got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:05.600
to be so Homer-like that they made it&nbsp;
the most-watched episode of the year.

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:10.960
After Homer gets a job as a medical test subject,&nbsp;
doctors find a crayon lodged in his brain. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:16.240
turns out that when Homer was a child he shoved&nbsp;
16 of them up his nose. He thought that he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:21.280
sneezed them all out but one stubborn crayon&nbsp;
stuck around for decades. After Homer has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:26.240
it surgically removed, his unencumbered&nbsp;
brain finally works correctly. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:31.440
Homer suddenly becomes intelligent, cultured,&nbsp;
and able to relate to brilliant Lisa for once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:36.640
They even go to the library together. However,&nbsp;
after a few ups and downs, Homer realizes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:41.600
he was much happier when he was less intelligent.&nbsp;
Luckily, Moe is more than willing to hammer the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:45.920
crayon back into his brain and restore&nbsp;
Homer's previous level of intelligence.

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:49.840
"Extended warranty? How can I lose?"

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:50.480
"Perfect."

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:57.200
"Large Marge" from 2002 is absolutely packed with&nbsp;
silly sight gags, which helped it become the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:02.160
popular episode of the year. For example,&nbsp;
when Lisa wins a bet with Homer and gets to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:06.640
choose their Daddy-Daughter Day activity, she&nbsp;
has them build houses for Domiciles for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:11.520
Destitute. They work alongside ex-presidents&nbsp;
Bill Clinton, George Bush and Jimmy Carter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:16.000
who engage in Three Stooges-esque&nbsp;
physical comedy and violent slapstick.

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:21.040
Later, at the worksite, Homer demonstrates to a&nbsp;
couple women how Marge birthed Bart — only for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:26.400
her to drive by and think that he is flirtatiously&nbsp;
flexing his muscles for them. Worried that Homer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:32.000
is losing his passion for her, Marge decides to&nbsp;
get liposuction. Unfortunately, the surgeons mix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:38.480
up their patients and provide the episode with its&nbsp;
central plotline and its most obvious sight gag.

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:42.320
"What on earth have you done?&nbsp;
My maguppies became bazongas!"

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:46.160
In 2003's "I'm Spelling as Fast as I Can,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:50.000
two big events simultaneously pass&nbsp;
through the greater Springfield area:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:54.720
A championship spelling competition and&nbsp;
a mania for Krusty Burger's Ribwich.

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:59.840
Of course, Homer gets addicted to the Ribwich, a&nbsp;
sandwich whose meat source is couched in secrecy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:04.480
He joins a group of "Ribheads" who travel&nbsp;
around to different Krusty Burger locations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:08.160
according to the sandwich's rolled-out&nbsp;
release schedule. Homer's new adventure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:13.040
in gluttony comes to an abrupt end when Krusty&nbsp;
announces that the Ribwich will be discontinued,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:16.640
as the animal from which it's&nbsp;
derived has suddenly gone extinct.

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:21.520
Meanwhile, Lisa quickly rises through the ranks&nbsp;
of competitive spelling. In a surprise twist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:26.720
writer George Plimpton asks Lisa to throw the&nbsp;
finals to a more popular and marketable competitor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:31.600
named Alex. If she goes along with the plan,&nbsp;
she'll receive a full college scholarship and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:36.560
a "George Plimpton Hot Plate." Lisa, of course,&nbsp;
sticks to her high morals and announces during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:42.080
the competition that she was asked to throw the&nbsp;
contest. She then defiantly continues to compete.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:46.080
Sadly, she then misspells her word&nbsp;
and knocks herself out of the finals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:50.240
Still, her family is incredibly proud&nbsp;
of Lisa and her second place finish.

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:53.040
"You're the number one speller in this car or in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:56.737
that car or in that car&nbsp;
or...don't look at that car."

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:01.520
Building fighting-robots is an ongoing&nbsp;
pastime for thousands of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:07.040
but in the early 2000s, the pursuit enjoyed a&nbsp;
mainstream moment with shows like BattleBots.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:12.800
The Simpsons tapped into this fad in 2004,&nbsp;
and it paid off. The robot-themed story "I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:16.400
(Annoyed Grunt)-Bot," , was the&nbsp;
most popular episode of the year.

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:20.000
After Homer does a terrible job&nbsp;
of assembling Bart's new bicycle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:25.440
he decides to make it up to him by constructing a&nbsp;
battle-bot to compete on the TV show Robot Rumble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:31.040
Homer's not the most talented engineer, however,&nbsp;
and resorts to crafting a glorified robot costume.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:35.200
He places himself inside, then goes&nbsp;
on television and suffers through many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:39.280
violent beatdowns until Bart finally&nbsp;
discovers what his dad has been up to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:43.840
But this doesn't stop Homer. He continues&nbsp;
to fight until the truth finally pops out.

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:49.840
"That robot has given birth to a man."

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:54.240
In 2005, Fox had the rights to the Super Bowl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:58.720
and gave The Simpsons the honor of airing&nbsp;
a special episode right after the big game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:03.760
That installment, "Homer and Ned's Hail Mary&nbsp;
Pass," seems like it was custom made to retain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:08.640
as many sports fans as possible. And it clearly&nbsp;
worked, since it was the year's most watched show.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:13.120
When a video of Homer doing a victory dance&nbsp;
after winning a carnival game goes viral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:17.600
several prominent athletes hire him to&nbsp;
choreograph their own celebratory dances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:21.840
LeBron James, Yao Ming, Michelle&nbsp;
Kwan, Warren Sapp, and Tom Brady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:25.680
all put in a request, and are all&nbsp;
voiced by their real-world selves.

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:29.920
Homer's dances grow into such a cultural&nbsp;
phenomenon that he wins a plum gig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:34.400
organizing the Super Bowl halftime show.&nbsp;
He recruits Ned Flanders to help him out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:39.120
seeing as how his pious neighbor has been making&nbsp;
a name for himself by producing violent biblical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:44.800
films. For the big halftime show, Homer and Ned&nbsp;
re-create the story of Noah's Ark for millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:49.920
football-loving fans. Of course, the reaction&nbsp;
of the viewing public was pretty predictable.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:53.920
"All over America today, viewers were&nbsp;
outraged by the Super Bowl Halftime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:56.560
Show's blatant display of religion and decency."

